---
layout: base
---

<!--
  A story's own page, and the last thing on the site Pagefind reads out of the
  rendered HTML. Everything else is a record built from the timeline collection
  (config/search.js), because a pic or a status is the whole of its own card. An
  essay is the opposite — the card is a teaser and the text is here — so
  `data-pagefind-body` puts the whole of it in the index and its timeline event
  is skipped over there.

  The filter is hardcoded because this layout has exactly one caller. `txt` is
  the section token the /search/ facet filters on, so a story and a status answer
  to the same checkbox the way they answer to the same nav item.

  The four meta keys are the ones config/search.js stamps on every custom
  record: /search/ draws a result as the card flattened, and an essay has to
  arrive carrying the same four or it is the one section that comes back looking
  like something else.

  `fulltext` is the fifth, and says the record's text is a page. Everything else
  in the index is the whole of its own card, so its description is all there is
  to show — but a hit in a story is usually a sentence four paragraphs down that
  neither the title nor the standfirst contains, so /search/ prints Pagefind's
  excerpt for these instead.

  **All four go through their own `data-` attribute, and that is not
  decoration.** `data-pagefind-meta` has two forms and only `key[attribute]`
  takes more than one pair; the inline `key:value` reads everything after the
  first colon as the value, comma and all. So `data-pagefind-meta="label:txt/blog,
  date:…"` is one key called `label` whose value is that entire string — which is
  what shipped, and what a result row printed where its section tag belonged.

  It is also why the thumbnail is further down on the <img>: a Cloudflare Images
  URL contains `width=352,format=auto`, which even the attribute form would split
  in half if the URL were written inline here.
-->
<main
  id="content"
  class="wrapper padding-x padding-y width-reading flex-grow"
  data-pagefind-body
  data-pagefind-filter="type:txt"
  data-label="txt/blog"
  data-fulltext="1"
  :data-date="dateHuman(date, 'day', undefined, true)"
  :data-datetime="dateMachine(date)"
  data-pagefind-meta="label[data-label], fulltext[data-fulltext], date[data-date], datetime[data-datetime]"
>
  <article class="content-body">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <span class="flex space-x-3">
        <p
          webc:for="item of filteredTags(tags)"
          class="tag !mb-6"
          @text="`#${item}`"
        ></p>
      </span>
      <h1 webc:if="title" @text="title"></h1>
      <!-- `data-pagefind-meta="desc"` is the element-content form: the standfirst
           is captured as the record's description and still renders here. It is
           what a story card prints under its heading, which makes it the same
           field config/search.js calls `desc` for every other kind of record. -->
      <p
        webc:if="standfirst"
        class="standfirst"
        data-pagefind-meta="desc"
        @text="standfirst"
      ></p>
      <time-date :date="date"></time-date>
    </header>
    <hr />
    <!-- Optional, and the same file the feed card draws — see `lead_image` in
         config/timeline.js. Under the rule rather than inside the header, so the
         header stays one block of who-what-when and the story opens on the picture.

         704 and 1408 deliberately: they are the pair <image-frame> already asks for,
         and config/media.js warns that Cloudflare bills per unique source × option
         string per month. Reusing them means the hero costs nothing the card has not
         already paid for. Only `sizes` differs — the reading column is max-w-4xl,
         not a 22rem card.

         `data-thumb` is a third width, and it is here rather than in the meta
         attribute on <main> for two separate reasons. A Cloudflare Images URL
         contains `width=352,format=auto`, and Pagefind splits that attribute on
         commas — the URL would arrive in halves. And `image[src]` would take the
         1408-wide hero for a thumbnail drawn at 5rem. 352 is the width
         <cover-art>, /job/ and the gallery thumbnails already ask for, which is
         the same `RESULT_WIDTH` config/search.js stamps on every other record:
         one more option string on an existing source, not a fourth transform. -->
    <img
      webc:if="lead_image"
      :src="imageUrl(lead_image, 1408)"
      :srcset="imageSrcset(lead_image, [704, 1408])"
      sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 56rem, 100vw"
      :width="imageShape(lead_image)?.width || false"
      :height="imageShape(lead_image)?.height || false"
      :alt="lead_alt || ''"
      :data-thumb="imageUrl(lead_image, 352)"
      data-pagefind-meta="image[data-thumb], image_alt[alt]"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <template webc:nokeep @html="content"></template>
    <code-highlight
      webc:nokeep
      webc:if="/<code class=&quot;language-/.test(content)"
    ></code-highlight>
  </article>
</main>
